# t0949 — the THIRD `Vector[Callable]` leak mechanism: the CONTAINER-ELEMENT
# INDEX READ materializes by CLONE into a temp that is never registered for
# drop, so every `fs[i]` read leaks a fresh closure environment.
#
# `ptr_materialization_kind` (`src/ir/lowering/context.rs`) answers
# `PtrMaterialization::Clone("gorget_closure_clone_to_owned")` for the pointer
# `gorget_array_get_at` returns, which mints a freshly-owned heap env — and
# nothing registers that temp for drop at its birth (Core #3). Emitted C:
#
#   __v18 = gorget_array_get_at(__v16, __v17, ...);
#   __v19 = gorget_closure_clone_to_owned(__v18);   <- fresh heap env
#   __s8  = __v19;                                  <- never `gorget_closure_free`d
#
# Measured with a heap-FORCED value: the closure CAPTURES `base`, so each env is
# a real 16-byte malloc rather than the 8-byte placeholder a non-capturing
# closure gets.
#
# ⭐ THE COUNT SCALES WITH THE NUMBER OF READS, NOT WITH THE NUMBER OF ELEMENTS,
# and that is what attributes the records. One element, two index reads:
#
#   | index reads | `.get()` bind in the program | leaked records |
#   |-------------|------------------------------|----------------|
#   | 2           | no                           | 1  (the pushed element only) |
#   | 1           | yes                          | 2  (pushed + 1 read temp)    |
#   | 2           | yes                          | 3  (pushed + 2 read temps)   |
#
# The `.get(0).unwrap()` bind is a PRECONDITION, not a leaker: it is what
# instantiates the `Named("Callable__GorgetClosure")` element type, after which
# the index read's materialization flips from a `DerefLoad` memcpy to a Clone.
# Its OWN clone temp is registered and freed (`gorget_closure_free(&__s17)` is
# emitted) — which is exactly why the index-read temp's absence is a defect and
# not a policy.
#
# ⭐ NO `.clone()` APPEARS ANYWHERE IN THIS PROGRAM, deliberately: the leak is
# not `todo/t0936`'s clone route. Verified against the PRE-`t0936` compiler
# (`git checkout 662e3a36f -- src/ir/lowering/exprs/methods.rs
# src/ir/lowering/context.rs`): the emitted C is BYTE-IDENTICAL and the same
# three records leak. The defect is older than the round that made it visible.
#
# INTENDED: prints 41, 42, 43 and is ASan-clean under `detect_leaks=1`, on C and
# LLVM. Measured at HEAD: `48 byte(s) leaked in 3 allocation(s)` on BOTH
# backends. (⚠ `scripts/sanitize_sweep.sh` is C-only, `todo/t0731` — the LLVM
# lane was checked by hand.)
#
# DISCRIMINATED from `t0873(b)`: that one is the CONTAINER's `elem_drop` never
# being synthesised, so the element the vector still holds is never freed at
# array teardown — one record per ELEMENT, at `src/lir/lower/insts.rs`. This one
# is a local temp in `main` that the container never held; synthesising
# `elem_drop` would not free it. Record #1 below is t0873(b)'s; records #2 and
# #3 are this item's.
# DISCRIMINATED from `t0948`: that one is a STRUCT's own drop strategy computed
# as `None` for a `Callable` field (`src/ir/types.rs:527`). No struct here.
# DISCRIMINATED from `t0406`: that is the element SIGNATURE collapsing to the
# zero-parameter fallback, a SEGV on the call. This program calls the element
# successfully and prints the right answers — it only leaks.

void main():
    int base = 40
    Vector[Callable[int(int)]] fs = []
    fs.push((int x): x + base)
    print(f"{fs[0](1)}")
    print(f"{fs[0](2)}")
    Callable[int(int)] g = fs.get(0).unwrap()
    print(f"{g(3)}")
